{
  "id": "COMPOUND:38301 - PROTEIN:5776",
  "verbalization": "Hedaol B is shown to have a target relationship with MIP through three distinct pathways, all of which involve the same chemical compound, (1S,3R,6S,8R,11S,12S,15R,16R)-15-[(2R)-4-[(2R)-3,3-dimethyloxiran-2-yl]butan-2-yl]-7,7,12,16-tetramethylpentacyclo[9.7.0.01,3.03,8.012,16]octadecan-6-ol. This compound is identified as the target of Hedaol B in three different cell lines: HepG2, A549, and SQ20B. The shared target molecule suggests a common mechanism of action across these targets, potentially involving the modulation of cellular processes that are influenced by this compound. The evidence supports a direct relationship between Hedaol B and MIP, as the same compound is consistently identified as the target in all three cases. The presence of a single, well-characterized target molecule across multiple cell lines strengthens the hypothesis that Hedaol B interacts with MIP through a common biological pathway. The mechanism of action is inferred to involve the inhibition or modulation of a cellular process that is affected by this compound, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
